---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { YouTube } from "astro-embed";
---

<Layout>
  <main class="flex flex-col w-full justify-center items-center">
    <div class="w-1/2 flex flex-col gap-10 my-[10vh]">
      <div class="header flex flex-col gap-5">
        <h1 class="text-4xl">Videos</h1>
        <p>
          Net neutrality has been around for decades, and advocates from all
          corners of the internet continue to fight for its preservation.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="videos flex flex-col gap-25">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
          <h2 class="text-2xl">Net Neutrality - Computerphile</h2>
          <YouTube id="SC-7fyBfvsw" />
          <p>Channel: Computerphile<br />Date: Nov 29, 2017</p>
          <p>
            The video features Professor Derek McAuley who explains that Net
            Neutrality is the principle that Internet Service Providers (ISPs)
            should not discriminate based on where data comes from, who is
            receiving it, or the type of "protocol" (the language the computers
            are using) being used. The video discusses the "bits are bits"
            principle, which means that all data on the internet should be
            treated equally, without discrimination. From a technical
            perspective, a packet of data is just a packet. Professor Derek
            McAuley compares it to a shipping parcel: you pay based on
            weight/volume, not based on whether the contents inside the box are
            a book or a diamond.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
          <h2 class="text-2xl">
            Net Neutrality: Last Week Tonight with John Oliver (HBO)
          </h2>
          <YouTube id="fpbOEoRrHyU" />
          <p>Channel: Last Week Tonight<br />Date: Jun 2, 2014</p>
          <p>
            In this segment, John Oliver explains that "Net Neutrality" is the
            simple principle that all internet data must be treated equally by
            providers. He argues that the FCC (at the time) was considering
            rules that would allow a "two-tier" system, effectively ending the
            open internet as we know it. John specifically talks about an
            incident where Comcast's negotiations with Netflix led to a slowdown
            in Netflix's streaming speeds for Comcast customers. He explains
            that this is an example of how ISPs could potentially charge
            companies for faster access to their customers, which would be
            detrimental to smaller companies and startups that cannot afford to
            pay for these "fast lanes."
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
          <h2 class="text-2xl">
            What to know about net neutrality as FCC weighs restoring it
          </h2>
          <YouTube id="YSvrFVZsUzU" />
          <p>
            Channel: CBS News<br />Date: Apr 3, 2024
          </p>
          <p>
            This video features NYU Law Professor Christopher Sprigman, who
            discusses the Biden Administration’s efforts to reinstate net
            neutrality rules that were removed during the Trump Administration.
            Professor Sprigman states that California already has passed their
            own net neutrality laws. Without net neutrality, Netflix for example
            could pay ISPs to prioritize their content, while smaller companies
            that cannot afford to pay for these "fast lanes" would be at a
            disadvantage. He also mentions that the internet is a critical
            infrastructure in today's world, and without net neutrality, it
            could lead to a less competitive market and harm consumers.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <hr />
      <div class="flex flex-row gap-5">
        <a
          href="/sources"
          class="px-4 py-2 border border-black hover:bg-black hover:text-white transition rounded"
          >Sources</a
        >
        <p class="text-center">---></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
